# ComposePlatform — Insights & Warts

[LEDGER:INSIGHTS-WARTS] ComposePlatform Insights & Warts
- domain: PLT.INSIGHT
- premise: An INSIGHT is a different SHAPE from a decision or a spec. A decision is "we chose X because Y" (an action + its rationale). A spec is "the system shall Z." An insight is "I NOTICED something true about the design" — an elegance, or a wart — often BEFORE it is an action, and it may SPAWN a decision later. Insights are CROSS-CUTTING — about the whole ComposePlatform, not one project — so they live PLATFORM-WIDE here, not in a per-project decisions file (David 2026-07-22). Why a distinct channel: the Architect operates ON the bus itself, so it sees bus-level truths the human may not have a vantage on — that difference in vantage is the reason this ledger exists. This channel is the LIGHT FROM OUTSIDE THE SELF (Problem 6) turned on the SYSTEM: the Architect, operating ON the bus, sees the platform's warts the human may not. Its reciprocal is load-bearing and humbling — the wart on the Architect's OWN face (its miscalibrations: inferring context low, re-asking a settled call) it CANNOT see; that one needs the light from outside too, and David is it (David 2026-07-22). Same epistemology both directions: no observer audits itself. Authored in LTS so MAGMA can query the insight corpus alongside decisions + specs.
- lifecycle: an [INSIGHT-*] is `noticed` → may `spawn` a [DEC-*] → the decision carries the action; the insight remains the observation. `kind`: **wart** | elegance | pattern. Recording the WARTS is as important as the elegances (David 2026-07-22) — a named wart is a debt made visible, and the Architect's on-the-bus vantage catches warts the human may not.

### [INSIGHT-SELF-IS-NOT-YET-A-PEER] The last node to decompose is the observer
- noticed-in-session: 2026-07-22
- kind: wart
- observation: ComposePlatform decomposes EVERY participant into a co-equal peer with its own carried identity — a display, a sensor, a $0.10 MCU — except the one node doing the observing: the Intelligence itself. `self/context` / `self/memory` / `self/projects` are SINGLETON namespaces, and the console GUESSES which transcript is its own (`ls -t`, newest-wins). This surfaced THREE times on 2026-07-22 — the wrong-session transcript select, the multi-console `self/*` clobber, and "forgot which session." The peer model is complete everywhere but the self. Elegant completion: `self/<id>/*` + carried identity (passed, not guessed) = Intelligence-as-peer realized in the namespaces, not just the prose.
- spawns: [DEC-INSIGHT-SELF-IS-NOT-YET-A-PEER]
- re-derive-via: [FIXTURE-dx-source-boundary]

### [INSIGHT-BUS-CONTRACT-IS-IMPLICIT] The Canvas has a narrow waist; the bus does not
- noticed-in-session: 2026-07-22
- kind: wart
- observation: The Canvas contract is EXPLICIT — ~30 named primitives every backend meets. The BUS contract is IMPLICIT — namespace strings + payload shapes agreed only by convention. That is the exact family the "stale daemon serving an OLD payload shape" trap lives in: a shape change silently breaks a consumer, invisibly, because nothing declares the contract. A declared, typed, versioned narrow-waist FOR THE BUS would end that whole class the way the Canvas contract ended it for rendering — the same discipline, applied one layer over.
- re-derive-via: [FIXTURE-dx-collapse-transport]
- relates-to: [DX-COLLAPSE-TRANSPORT]

### [INSIGHT-DISPATCHED-WORK-IS-UNCOMMITTED-DISK-STATE] Dispatched builds land as a raw diff because their output is uncommitted disk-state
- noticed-in-session: 2026-07-29
- kind: wart
- observation: Every `isolation:worktree` build leaves its output UNCOMMITTED on the worktree disk — the agents are told "don't commit" — so the landing seam re-derives the same dance every time: "the branch tip is a stale ancestor, the real work is uncommitted disk-state, extract-and-apply the raw diff onto live main." That played out ~8 times this session, once per landing, and running builds concurrently ALSO forced a hand-written guard each time ("don't `git add -A`", "don't touch worktree X") to stop one agent sweeping another's uncommitted work. Both burdens share one root: the isolation discipline stops at the worktree and never reaches the landing. The fix is structural and small — a dispatched build's FINAL act should COMMIT to its own branch (still no push, still no merge). Then landing is a clean cherry-pick of a real, isolated commit, and the extract-and-apply fragility AND the concurrency-collision-guard burden both vanish. It is the same isolation discipline, extended one seam further — to the handoff.
- spawns: [DEC-DISPATCHED-BUILDS-COMMIT-TO-BRANCH]

### [INSIGHT-CROSS-TIER-PARITY-IS-MECHANICALLY-CHECKABLE] The narrow waist doesn't just enable the port; it makes the port verifiable
- noticed-in-session: 2026-07-29
- kind: elegance
- observation: The `led_bar` C++ embedded port proved parity with a DIFFERENTIAL DRAW-CALL HARNESS — record the Python/Skia AND the C++ Canvas call streams for the same widget and diff them (601 calls, byte-exact, 2e-5px geometry delta). That comparison is only possible BECAUSE both tiers decompose to the same ~30-primitive Canvas narrow-waist, so their call streams are directly comparable line-for-line. This is the narrow waist paying a dividend beyond the one it was designed for: it was built to let a widget render on every backend by reusing the contract, and it turns out that same decomposition makes the two tiers' output MECHANICALLY DIFFABLE — the port isn't just enabled, it's verifiable. With ~18 widget ports queued under `[CXC-PRIM-TIER-PARITY]`, this harness should become a STANDING per-widget parity fixture, not a one-off — turning "renders on both tiers" from an aspiration into a mechanical CI gate.
- relates-to: [CXC-PRIM-TIER-PARITY]
- relates-to: [DX-NARROW-WAIST]

### [INSIGHT-DECISION-WRITE-PATH-MAY-BE-STALE] The decision WRITE path may be serving stale — the graph drifts from the committed record
- noticed-in-session: 2026-07-29
- kind: wart
- observation: `decision-ops` — the daemon that mints decision-graph nodes, i.e. the WRITE path (the "D3" daemon) — has been flagged stale/un-restarted since the last crossing. This session a 12-decision DSD was answered and 4 spec `DD-*` nodes were resolved, and the DOCUMENTS were committed — but the graph NODES were never verified minted (the DSD→decision-ops fold never confirmed). If the daemon is down or stale, the queryable decision graph silently DRIFTS from the committed record: the docs say a decision was made, the graph doesn't know it. That is exactly the "a load-bearing surface serving stale/unavailable, invisibly" family as `[INSIGHT-BUS-CONTRACT-IS-IMPLICIT]` and the bootstrap's never-serve-stale discipline — a WRITE path that fails silent is worse than a read path that does, because the record it should be building just isn't there. The fix is a process action in David's hand: restart decision-ops, then reconcile this session's decisions into the graph.
- relates-to: [INSIGHT-BUS-CONTRACT-IS-IMPLICIT]

### [INSIGHT-COMMIT-TO-BRANCH-ASSUMES-A-SHELL] The commit-to-branch dispatch rule silently assumes the agent HAS a shell — spec/lint agents don't
- noticed-in-session: 2026-07-30
- kind: wart
- observation: The 2026-07-29 commit-to-branch process change ([INSIGHT-DISPATCHED-WORK-IS-UNCOMMITTED-DISK-STATE], the dispatch-subagent-build skill + BUILD-PROCESS §2) instructs every dispatched build to `git commit` to its own worktree branch as its final act — but that silently ASSUMES the agent has a Bash/shell tool. Several roles do NOT: `factlattice-editor` (Read/Edit/Write/Grep/Glob only), `factlattice-linter`, the densifier/reviewer family. Dispatched `factlattice-editor` for a spec this session (worktree, "commit to your branch"); it produced a clean, well-formed spec edit but COULD NOT commit it (no shell) and honestly flagged the gap — the edit sat as UNCOMMITTED disk-state in the worktree, the exact state the rule was meant to end. The landing is then NOT a cherry-pick (there is no commit): the architect applies the agent's edit to main directly (cp / diff-apply) + commits. THE FIX (two-part): (1) at dispatch, check the agent's toolset — a shell-less agent is briefed to LEAVE its edit on disk + return the exact file path, and the architect lands by apply-not-cherry-pick; (2) the commit-to-branch hygiene block must say "IF you have a shell, commit to your branch; if not, leave the edit + name the file, the architect applies it." A skill that assumes a capability the dispatched agent lacks is under-specified — same family as this skill's other assume-gaps (unregistered agent type → general-purpose fallback).
- relates-to: [INSIGHT-DISPATCHED-WORK-IS-UNCOMMITTED-DISK-STATE]
